  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The lace square keeps the four corner (3 "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "treble"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "tr"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", ch 2, 3 tr) structure that defines a square shape and replaces the side "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "treble-cluster"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "treble clusters"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " with chain-3 arches connected by "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "slip-stitch"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "slip stitches"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". The sides alternate between arch and cluster rows: round 2 replaces clusters with arches, round 3 fills arches with "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "double-crochet-uk"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "double crochet"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " clusters, round 4 replaces those clusters with arches again. The result is a light, open side with a solid corner anchor. Chain stitches work at roughly three times the speed of trebles, so the open sides are surprisingly fast."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "infoPanel",
        "attrs": {
          "variant": "materials"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Yarn: DK weight cotton, approximately 26 m per motif. Hook: 4 mm. Notions: tapestry needle, scissors, blocking mat, blocking pins."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gauge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "4 rounds in DK cotton with a 4 mm hook = 11 x 11 cm after "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "blocking"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "blocking"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". The chain arches open substantially when pinned; the blocked size will be noticeably larger than the unblocked size. Always measure after blocking when planning a project using this motif."
          }
        ]
